art take the and when you spot papers which are very close to what we do take the time to read it and to try to understand what is the the common structure that is across all these papers. Yes. But otherwise you will find a resource or example of what should contain uh the production the related work etc. I see some example here but they are not the one I I had in mind. Yeah, perfect. Okay. So, anything else that I see? I mean, that's clear. Okay. Yeah. Uh well, I will share with you in the chat, but that's one among many I guess other sources about how to write scientific paper. So go through it, you will see that the paper is always very dry, very factual. You don't claim anything that you cannot either prove or reference or so you can prove by mathematics or you can prove by experiments or you have a reference which have done the experiment for you or the proof for you and you cite it. You never claim something with in the vacuum. It's not an opinion, right? And you avoid every word like oh it's extraordinary or it's smart or it's beautiful or all this unsubstantiated play vague should not should be avoided but it's usually very dry. It's not like literature and it must be very clear, very when you name something, you keep that name all across the paper to avoid confusion because you have to think that people who read your paper never read about your research before and they don't know what you talk about and they are you are not here to explain them. They rely on entirely on the reading the paper. So it has to be extremely clear and very easy to that's sometime not easy at all but step by step explaining things and being very very short sentences no complex grammar blah blah blah we usually are we are not English native we usually do not go into that direction anyway but short sentence very simple claim and very simple things You will see. Yes. Okay. All right. Thank you so much. Okay. Great. Uh we will target based on the where they are being published. We will