		{"id":"2fca1ef8ec3cfdeb","type":"text","text":"","x":2226,"y":529,"width":854,"height":551},
		{"id":"44b1e41ba616ea71","type":"text","text":"Here are the most technical parts, pulled out and cleaned into “what matters for the system + paper”.\n\n## 1) End to end pipeline (core technical idea)\n\n- Input NLQ (natural language question)\n    \n- Paraphrase NLQ using an LLM\n    \n- Feed paraphrase into NL to SQL model to get predicted SQL (prime SQL)\n    \n- Verify predicted SQL is equivalent to ground truth SQL using a formal SQL equivalence checker\n    \n- If equivalent, accept paraphrase as semantically correct\n    \n- Then run diversity scoring to pick the most diverse paraphrase(s) among the correct ones\n    \n\nKey point: semantic correctness is checked first (binary pass or fail), diversity is only judged after correctness is confirmed.\n\n## 2) SQL equivalence verification (the “semantic correctness” gate)\n\nTool discussed: QED (Query Equivalence Decider for SQL), based on bag semantics (multiset semantics, duplicates matter).\n\nInputs needed:\n\n- Two SQL queries (predicted vs ground truth)\n    \n- One schema (same schema used for both queries)\n    \n- Optional integrity constraints and NULL logic related constraints\n    \n\nOutput:\n\n- Equivalent or not equivalent (plus internal proof style reasoning via its formalism)\n    \n\nImportant claims made:\n\n- It checks equivalence beyond just “same output on one database”\n    \n- It can prove equivalence for queries that look structurally different but are semantically the same\n    \n- Uses “bag semantics” so duplicates are handled correctly\n    \n\nPractical notes mentioned:\n\n- Tool exists publicly\n    \n- There may be implementation constraints (Rust and Java mentioned)\n    \n- Needs checking for edge cases and coverage limits (because papers report it cannot verify every query pair)\n    \n\n## 3) Why not just compare query outputs on a database\n\nThe discussion contrasts:\n\n- Naive method: run both queries on a database instance and compare outputs\n    \n- Problem: one DB instance is not a proof, plus duplicates and NULL behavior complicate it\n    \n- QED aims to reason formally about equivalence, not just empirical matching\n    \n\n## 4) Sentence embedding similarity baseline (competitor for semantic equivalence)\n\nBaseline discussed: SBERT style sentence embeddings + cosine similarity\n\nProcess:\n\n- Encode two sentences into vectors\n    \n- Compute cosine similarity\n    \n- High score means “similar meaning” (in theory)\n    \n\nKnown failure modes (technical limitations):\n\n- Negation flips meaning but similarity can stay high  \n    Example: “dog chased man” vs “dog did not chase man”\n    \n- Quantities change meaning but similarity can stay high  \n    Example: “population is 5 million” vs “population is 50 million”\n    \n- Logical quantifiers can break it  \n    Example: “all students passed” vs “some students passed”\n    \n\nConclusion from the meeting:\n\n- SBERT is a valid baseline to cite for semantic similarity, but it is unreliable as a semantic equivalence verifier for your use case.\n    \n\n## 5) Diversity scoring baselines\n\nMetrics mentioned:\n\n- Jaccard similarity (lexical overlap, order insensitive)\n    \n- BLEU (n-gram overlap, order sensitive)\n    \n\nKey reasoning:\n\n- Diversity is basically “low similarity” after correctness is guaranteed\n    \n- You can treat diversity score as 1 minus similarity\n    \n- But BLEU and Jaccard must be tested only on semantically equivalent pairs (otherwise the comparison is meaningless)\n    \n\n## 6) Evaluation plan (how to pick thresholds and prove usefulness)\n\n- Use SQL equivalence as the main automatic semantic filter\n    \n- Rank surviving paraphrases by diversity metric (Jaccard, BLEU, etc.)\n    \n- Human validation on a sample:\n    \n    - For each original question, inspect top k diverse paraphrases that passed equivalence\n        \n    - Judge if they are actually correct and diverse\n        \n- Then measure correlation between human diversity judgments and automatic diversity scores\n    \n- Use that to justify your ranking approach in the paper (and potentially avoid manual threshold tuning)\n    \n\n## 7) Literature review and competitor selection method (systematic)\n\n- Focus on last 2 to 3 years for competitors (2024 to 2026 ideally)\n    \n- First pass filter by title, second pass by abstract\n    \n- Build a table with columns like:\n    \n    - Task type: general paraphrase vs NLQ, question vs answer paraphrasing\n        \n    - Semantic verification method: embeddings, entailment, SQL based, formal equivalence, etc.\n        \n    - Diversity metric used\n        \n    - Same language vs cross language\n        \n    - Requires database or not\n        \n- Target 3 to 5 strong baselines if the area is crowded, otherwise 2 to 3 if niche\n    \n\n## 8) Diagram corrections requested (technical communication)\n\n- Make arrows and layout simpler (avoid unnecessary curved arrows)\n    \n- SQL equivalence box should clearly show:\n    \n    - Inputs (GT SQL, predicted SQL, schema, constraints)\n        \n    - Output (equivalent or not)\n        \n- Add a “status view” version of the diagram:\n    \n    - what is implemented, what is partial, what is missing\n        \n\nIf you want, I can turn this into:\n\n- a 1 page “system spec” (inputs outputs assumptions failure modes), or\n    \n- a short “Methods” section draft for your paper (with placeholders for citations).","x":1740,"y":2040,"width":1406,"height":1400},
